let's go back to summer of 2012 Sheryl  Sandberg has just announced she'll be  publishing her first book lean in the  book is called  as a call to action to women everywhere  to lean into their careers and rise with  the ranks of Corporate America before  the book is even published there's  numerous responses in the media  including an article titled why women  still can't have it all written by  anne-marie slaughter former senior  official at the State Department  slaughter writes about the near  impossibility of women to balance a  demanding career and motherhood in the  work environment as it currently stands  a few months later I received my  acceptance letter to Stanford Graduate  School of Business and the margins of  the letter Derrick had written in  cardinal red ink Jennifer the lessons  your mother imparted on you will ensure  that you contribute grow and learn at  Stanford because see when Stanford asked  me what matters most to you and why it  was the easiest question undoubtedly my  mom who equipped with the master's  degree dedicated her life to raising my  two siblings of me there were 22 schools  in Spain Mexico Korea Hong Kong and the  all to exotic state of New Jersey so  there I was so excited of having just  been accepted to the GSB and yet there  is a sense of confusion in the media was  this ongoing debate not simply on how  but whether women could balance a career  and motherhood and yet the very reason I  was accepted into this institution was  because of a woman who had never held a  title other than Allah or mom I asked  myself where was my mom's voice in this  debate on how I'm supposed to lead a  life balancing career and motherhood  which brings us to today here at the GSB  where I learned there's a number of  alumni female alums who question whether  the GSB should be accepting as many  women given the number of alumni who  have  dropped out of the workforce to be clear  this is not a GSB problem when asked to  comment for an article in The New York  Times the former Director of Admissions  at Harvard questioned what return do we  expect from educating women The Guardian  chided a little more bluntly that  educated women have a duty to stay in  the workforce all these arguments over  value the return generated by  professional achievements while  undervaluing the role of full-time  mothers and also dismissing the value  that we women currently in schools bring  to a diverse learning environment to say  the least it struck a chord and I'm  thrilled to be here speaking to you all  today in defense of my mom's decision  and the decision of countless women who  chose to become stay-at-home moms now I  realize I'm about to step into a  minefield as this topic of educated  stay-at-home moms touches on  socio-economic issues gender dynamics  parenting values just to name a few and  I encourage all of you to explore all of  these nuances before today I want to  focus on us our GSP community and to be  clear I'm not trying to advocate for a  certain decision but merely to lend this  stage to the countless voices that go  unheard because their arena of work  isn't the most private of private  sectors their homes in preparing for  today I had the privilege of speaking  with women from the class of 79 to 2005  learning about their experiences as  full-time moms their stories were as  unique as they were stereotypical LuAnn  GSB class of 79 during our time at home  organized professional development for  the young presidents organization served  as auditor of her children's PTA and  worked with the Children's Health  Institute and San Francisco Symphony now  how is her passion for all this  volunteer work any different from our  ambitions of becoming leaders and  mentors and organized  but then there was Tracy class of 98 who  vowed never to become a stay-at-home mom  since she was 10 years old and her  father unexpectedly left her family  leaving her mother as a sudden  breadwinner but after several  miscarriages her dream of becoming a  mother became contingent upon her  leaving her career and nurturing her  first child from the moment of  conception I asked Stephanie class of  2001 what impact her GSB education has  had on her and she confided the finance  I don't use every day but the soft  skills I learned I use all the time and  is this any different from what we hear  from the countless CEO alums who come  back to campus they told me stories of  early frustration where they long for  the intellectual challenges of the  careers they left behind spending days  with the child who couldn't walk nor  talk let alone walk the talk but these  days would be balanced by in comparably  rewarding days where they were witness  to their child's first steps and first  words seeing the impact of their endless  encouragement speaking with these women  has been an absolute blast they have  given me the confidence that right now  without a ring or child I have no  decision to make but their voices are  loud and clear in my head that should  anyone choose to work full-time as a  mother it is an entirely individual  respectable and fulfilling decision so  then who are we as members of the GSB  community and broader society to be  alarmed or disappointed by these women  who are changing lives of the leaders of  our future changing organizations  whether as mom and chief we're head of a  non-profit and is changing this world  each and every day if I were to ask a  roomful of people say you all  who is the person you most admire we're  sitting here in Silicon Valley maybe  someone will say Steve Jobs roomful of  MBAs perhaps we'll get a Warren Buffett  maybe we'll even get the leader of this  nation and I'll wager although each by a  different name  a handful myself included will say it is  their moms that they most admire so why  don't we give our women our alumni our  peers who choose to dedicate themselves  to the noble profession of motherhood  the respect and admiration they so  rightfully deserve thank you
 

